\subsection{The maximum rank over all classes}

\begin{conjecture}\label{conj:max}
On $K_n$, the maximum of the Baker--Norine rank over all divisor classes of degree
$d\ge0$ is attained at the concentrated divisor $d\cdot v$ for any single vertex $v$, and
equals
\[
  \rho_n(d)=\frac{a(a+1)}{2}+\min(b,a),\qquad d=a(n-1)+b,\ 0\le b\le n-2 .
\]
\end{conjecture}

At $d=g-1$ this predicts $0,0,2,2,5,5,9,9,14,14,\dots$ for $n=3,4,5,\dots$, each value
$j(j+3)/2$ repeated twice.  Exhaustive verification for $n\le6$ confirms both the maximum
and that the concentrated divisor attains it; the predicted value is confirmed for the
concentrated divisor at $n=7$ as well.  A consequence, if the conjecture holds, is that
$K_n$ meets the universal half-canonical target $k-1=n-2$ with equality only at $n=7$:
the maximum is strictly below the target for $n\le6$ and for $n=8$, equal at $n=7$, and
strictly above from $n=9$ on, since $\rho_n(g-1)\sim n^2/8$ outgrows $n-2$.

\subsection{The residual regularities $k=5$ and $k=7$}

The universal half-canonical existence theorem covers $k\ge6$, $k\ne7$.  At $k=5$,
exhaustive enumeration of all $3125$ divisor classes of degree $g-1=9$ on the $5$-regular
graph $K_6$ shows the maximum rank there is $2<4=k-1$; hence any statement of the form
``every $5$-regular graph on at least $N_0$ vertices carries a divisor of degree $g-1$
and rank $\ge4$'' requires $N_0>6$.  At $k=7$, on $K_8$ (degree $g-1=20$) every tested
witness has rank exactly $5=k-2$, one short of the target.  These are precisely the two
regularities for which $\lfloor (k-2)/2\rfloor\in\{1,2\}$ and the one-shot estimates fall
short.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\subsection{What made the complete graph tractable}

Three structural facts conspired.  First, \emph{rank-one coupling}: on $K_n$ the
Laplacian is $nI-J$ with $J$ all-ones, so the off-diagonal part has rank one and a firing
vector is summarised, from each vertex's viewpoint, by a single scalar.  Second,
\emph{convexity and periodicity}: the resulting objective $\varphi_D$ is a piecewise
linear function of one integer variable whose increments depend only on residues mod $n$,
hence periodic and minimisable by a linear scan.  Third, a \emph{fixed-point structure on
thresholds}: the lower bound needed not merely a good threshold but the minimal one,
which is automatically an equilibrium $T(t)=t$ --- a discrete analogue of the least
fixed point in Dhar's burning algorithm \cite{Dhar}, and what makes the winning vector
explicit.

\subsection{Open problems}

Conjecture~\ref{conj:max} is the most concrete; its truth would determine the
Brill--Noether behaviour of complete graphs completely.

\emph{Extension of the criterion.}  Which graph families admit an effectivity criterion
with few parameters?  Complete multipartite graphs have Laplacians of the form
(diagonal) minus (low rank), so it is plausible that the dollar game there reduces to an
optimisation in as many integer parameters as there are parts.  Strongly regular graphs
and circulants with few distinct eigenvalues are further candidates; each such reduction
would yield exact rank formulas for a new infinite family.

\emph{The two residual regularities.}  Is there a finite $N_0(5)$ such that every simple
connected $5$-regular graph on at least $N_0(5)$ vertices carries a divisor of degree
$g-1$ and rank at least $4$, and likewise $N_0(7)$ for rank $6$?  The $K_6$ computation
shows $N_0(5)>6$, so a genuine threshold is required.  Near-uniform witnesses on
$5$-regular circulants appear to have rank exactly $2$ regardless of size, so any proof
must select the witness using the structure of the graph.

\emph{Scale of the threshold.}  The exact positivity criterion for the Brill--Noether
number at $(d,r)=(g-1,k-1)$ on a $k$-regular graph on $n$ vertices is $2k^2\le(k-2)n$,
i.e.\ $n\gtrsim 2k+4$; a sufficient linear bound $n\ge2k+7$ is available.  The plausible
sharp threshold is $\Theta(k)$, not $\Theta(k^2)$.

\emph{Theta characteristics as extremal witnesses.}  For every even $k\ge6$, is the
maximum of the rank over classes of degree $g-1$ on a $k$-regular graph attained at a
theta characteristic?  On $K_{2m+3}$ this is what happens.  Since theta characteristics
form a finite set of classes, this would replace a search over $\Pic^{g-1}$ by a search
over a computable finite set.

\subsection{Beyond graphs}

Baker's specialisation lemma \cite{Baker} relates the rank of a divisor on an algebraic
curve to that of its specialisation on the dual graph of a degeneration:
$r_{\text{curve}}\le r_{\text{graph}}$.  Theorem~\ref{thm:main} therefore yields
nontrivial upper bounds for ranks of divisors on curves degenerating to a configuration
whose dual graph is complete --- common for stable curves with many pairwise-meeting
components --- and the independence of $n$ makes the bound uniform in the number of
components.

\section{Summary of results}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Effectivity criterion.}  On $K_n$, $D\sim$ effective iff
$\exists s:\sum_v\lceil (s-D(v))/n\rceil\le s$; the optimal $s$ yields the firing vector
explicitly.
\item \textbf{Linear-time decision.}  The deficiency $d(D)$ is computable in $O(n)$ from
the residue histogram of $D$.
\item \textbf{Riemann's inequality on $K_n$.}  Every divisor of degree $\ge g$ is
equivalent to an effective divisor; the staircase divisor $(-1,0,1,\dots,n-2)$ of degree
$g-1$ shows sharpness.
\item \textbf{Counting identity.}
$\sum_{j<m}\lceil (a-j)/n\rceil_+=m\lfloor a/n\rfloor+\min(m,a\bmod n)\le a$, strictly if
$a>m$ (for $m+2\le n$).
\item \textbf{Exact rank.}  $r(m\cdot\one)=\frac{m(m+3)}{2}$ on $K_n$ for all $n\ge m+2$;
independent of $n$, quadratic in $m$, constructively certified in $O(nm)$ time.
\item \textbf{One-shot insufficiency.}  For $m\ge3$ the exact rank strictly exceeds
$2m+\lfloor m^2/4\rfloor$.
\item \textbf{Theta characteristics.}  On $K_{2m+3}$ the divisor $m\cdot\one$ satisfies
$2D=K$ exactly, has degree $g-1$ and rank $\frac{m(m+3)}{2}$, beating the universal
half-canonical guarantee $k-1$ quadratically and satisfying $4r>g$.
\end{itemize}

